// Practice quiz: Reading code coverage
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, so comparisons are written in words.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/testing-and-java-review/code-coverage

::o1 a red line::In a JaCoCo HTML report, what does a red line mean? {
=None of the line's instructions ran during the tests#Correct. Red means not covered, yellow means some instructions ran, and green means all of them did.
~A test failed on that line#Not quite. Coverage colors show what ran, not which tests passed or failed.
~Only some of the line's instructions ran#Not quite. That is a yellow line.
~The line contains a bug#Not quite. Coverage cannot see bugs; it only records what executed.
}

::o1 one branch missed::An if line shows a yellow diamond with the hint 1 of 2 branches missed. What does it tell you? {
=The tests sent the condition only one way, so one outcome never happened#Correct. The line ran, but a test with inputs that go the other way is still missing.
~The line never ran#Not quite. A line that never ran is red, with a red diamond.
~One of the tests on that line failed#Not quite. Branch coverage counts outcomes taken, not test results.
~The condition can never be true#Not quite. It only means no test made it go that way yet.
}

::o2 no assertion::A test calls every method of a class but asserts nothing, and coverage reaches 100 percent. What does that show? {
=Only that the code ran without throwing an exception#Correct. With no assertion, the test passes whatever the methods return.
~That the class returns correct results#Not quite. Nothing compared any result with an expected value.
~Nothing, because JUnit fails a test with no assertion#Not quite. JUnit passes a test that finishes without an exception.
~That the class has no untested branches left to worry about#Not quite. Every branch ran, but none of their results were checked.
}

::o2 wrong expected value::A test expects the wrong value, and the code happens to return that same wrong value. How does coverage show this? {
=Exactly like a correct test: the lines are covered and the problem is invisible#Correct. Coverage counts executed code the same way whether the assertion is right, wrong or missing.
~The asserted line turns red#Not quite. The line ran, so it is covered.
~The asserted line turns yellow#Not quite. Yellow means only some instructions ran, which is not the case here.
~JaCoCo flags the wrong expected value#Not quite. JaCoCo never reads what an assertion expects.
}

::o3 cover the false branch::discount(qty) returns 10 when qty is at least 100, otherwise 0. The only test uses qty 150. Which new test covers the missed branch? {
=assertEquals(0, discount(5));#Correct. 5 sends the condition the other way, and the test still asserts the expected result.
~assertEquals(10, discount(200));#Not quite. 200 takes the same branch as 150.
~A test that calls discount(5) and asserts nothing#Not quite. It takes the missed branch but proves nothing about the result.
~assertEquals(10, discount(100));#Not quite. 100 is at least 100, so it takes the branch already covered.
}

::o3 two separate ifs::A method has two separate if statements, and its one test makes both conditions true. What is the fewest extra tests that takes all four branches? {
=One more test that makes both conditions false#Correct. The first test takes both true branches, and the new one takes both false branches.
~Three more tests, one for each remaining combination#Not quite. Branch coverage needs each outcome once, not every combination.
~None, because both lines already ran#Not quite. Each if still has one outcome that was never taken.
~Two more tests, one for each if#Not quite. One test can send both conditions the false way at once.
}
